  "slug": "ai-topic-c4ec",
  "summary": "New benchmarks show limited cross-source drug-response concordance and prior-dependent predictability, challenging how perturbation models are trained and evaluated.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is auditing the data, priors, and confounding structure that perturbation- and drug-response models depend on, rather than introducing new predictors. A benchmark of six drug-perturbation transcriptomic resources reports limited cross-source concordance and identifies drug main-effect contributions. A gene-similarity framework comparing eight prior sources with Perturb-seq finds that prior agreement tracks target-level predictability. Resource work adds four patient-derived organoid drug screens with multi-omics for training and benchmarking, plus a community benchmark of 11 generative methods for synthetic bulk RNA-seq. Methodological papers target confounding and disentanglement: a normalized HSIC kernel regularizer for single-cell counterfactuals and SCOUT for ATE/CATE estimation with noisy proxies. A benchmark of precision matrix estimators for differential co-expression networks fits the same evaluation-focused theme. Collectively, reported performance appears contingent on dataset concordance, prior quality, and confounder handling.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-15"
}
